model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The OAuth review registry is the one place that marks a connection as asking for its issuer's whole published scope list (scopeRequest issuer-list). Upstream preparation generates the SDK's whole-list issuers from it, each with the issuer's reviewed list and a baseline frozen when the connection was marked, and refuses two marks for one issuer however it is spelled; the source check refuses a generated list that differs, and catalog tests fail when a marked connection's requested scopes differ from its issuer's reviewed list, when a connection those tests hold to its issuer's list is not marked, or when a pinned baseline changes, and preparation refuses a marked connection without its baseline. A sign-in to a marked issuer that asks for every baseline scope the issuer still publishes records so on its grant, and renewals by releases that know the record keep it; a release that does not know it ignores it, and its grants then count only through the baseline. A grant that recorded it, or that asked for every baseline scope, is not held to a scope it did not ask for that is on the issuer's reviewed list or was dropped from the baseline: the issuer's list growing, also while the provider still asks for the shorter list, neither marks the account as needing new permissions nor refuses a shared grant at credential use, and the next sign-in or reconnect asks for what the issuer publishes. A scope outside both lists, or one the grant asked for and lacks, stays required. Every other grant is held to every requested scope: other issuers, grants that asked for less (the three-scope grants from before full access, narrower apps), grants that recorded no request, first-party and client-credentials grants. So an app that adds a scope the issuer does not publish, or any app on another grant, still asks its accounts to reconnect, and connecting another app through an existing grant still asks for consent when the grant lacks what that app declares. A stored provider keeps the longer scope list it once requested, so rolling the host back past a release that grew a reviewed list holds grants to scopes the older list no longer names."
    }
  }
}
